<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" standalone="yes"?><ns2:section xmlns:ns2="http://rest.cis.illinois.edu" id="58958" href="schedule/2022/spring/ACE/199/58958.xml"><parents><calendarYear id="2022" href="schedule/2022.xml">2022</calendarYear><term id="120221" href="schedule/2022/spring.xml">Spring 2022</term><subject id="ACE" href="schedule/2022/spring/ACE.xml">Agricultural and Consumer Economics</subject><course id="199" href="schedule/2022/spring/ACE/199.xml">Undergraduate Open Seminar</course></parents><sectionNumber>SFS</sectionNumber><sectionTitle>Sustainable Food Systems</sectionTitle><creditHours>3 hours</creditHours><statusCode>A</statusCode><sectionText>Sustainable food systems are economically viable, environmentally sound, and socially just. In this course, we will examine the economic, environmental, and social aspects of mainstream food systems. We will then explore the opportunities and challenges for alternative models of food production and consumption. We will use case studies of recent food system sustainability initiatives and success stories and interact with leaders involved in such efforts in multiple food sectors.</sectionText><partOfTerm>1</partOfTerm><sectionStatusCode>A</sectionStatusCode><enrollmentStatus>UNKNOWN</enrollmentStatus><startDate>2022-01-18Z</startDate><endDate>2022-05-04Z</endDate><meetings><meeting id="0"><type code="LCD">Lecture-Discussion</type><start>12:00 PM</start><end>12:50 PM</end><daysOfTheWeek>MWF    </daysOfTheWeek><roomNumber>23</roomNumber><buildingName>ACES Lib, Info &amp; Alum Ctr</buildingName><instructors><instructor lastName="Atallah" firstName="S">Atallah, S</instructor></instructors></meeting></meetings></ns2:section>